1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:03,792
Our prover works in a field of 31 bits.

2
00:00:03,842 --> 00:00:10,114
The signature we want to verify, PLUM, computes in a prime field of 199 bits.

3
00:00:11,014 --> 00:00:13,907
Take one multiplication in that big field.

4
00:00:13,957 --> 00:00:17,376
A prover built on the big field would do it in one step.

5
00:00:17,426 --> 00:00:19,783
That is the cost we would like to pay.

6
00:00:20,683 --> 00:00:25,010
Our prover must split each number into 7 limbs of 30 bits.

7
00:00:25,060 --> 00:00:31,804
Schoolbook multiplication then forms 7 times 7, or 49, limb products.

8
00:00:33,004 --> 00:00:39,934
One PLUM verification at the 80-bit level calls the Griffin hash 1052 times.

9
00:00:39,984 --> 00:00:44,320
Griffin is built from exactly these big-field multiplications.

10
00:00:45,220 --> 00:00:51,365
Run as plain software, the verification takes 7.08 billion guest cycles.

11
00:00:51,415 --> 00:00:58,262
On a 24 gigabyte laptop, the prover runs out of memory after about 1 minute 45 seconds.

12
00:00:59,162 --> 00:01:01,649
The standard fix is a precompile.

13
00:01:01,699 --> 00:01:07,677
It is a dedicated circuit for one operation, called from the program like a system call.

14
00:01:07,727 --> 00:01:10,504
We built one for the Griffin permutation.

15
00:01:11,404 --> 00:01:16,420
With it, the proof finishes in 14.25 minutes, the mean of 3 runs.

16
00:01:16,470 --> 00:01:18,640
So far the story is simple.

17
00:01:18,690 --> 00:01:20,962
The precompile makes it faster.

18
00:01:21,862 --> 00:01:23,789
Now swap the hash instead.

19
00:01:23,839 --> 00:01:30,260
A control arm uses SHA-3, computed in ordinary software, with no hash precompile at all.

20
00:01:31,160 --> 00:01:34,995
The control finishes in 13.05 minutes.

21
00:01:35,045 --> 00:01:38,822
The arm with the precompile is slower, in every run we took.

22
00:01:39,722 --> 00:01:43,125
A precompile removes cycles from the program.

23
00:01:43,175 --> 00:01:48,392
But its own circuit still has to be proved, and its rows are not counted as cycles.

24
00:01:49,592 --> 00:01:54,135
The prover commits a table of cells, and its work grows with the number of cells.

25
00:01:54,185 --> 00:01:56,518
We call that number the trace area.

26
00:01:57,418 --> 00:01:59,844
A precompile changes two terms.

27
00:01:59,894 --> 00:02:04,394
It removes core area, the cells the removed cycles would have used.

28
00:02:04,444 --> 00:02:07,419
It adds chip area, the cells of its own circuit.

29
00:02:08,319 --> 00:02:15,140
It pays exactly when the core area removed exceeds the chip area added, each weighted by its cost per cell.

30
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:18,333
Check it against plain software.

31
00:02:18,383 --> 00:02:22,784
Each Griffin call removes about 6.6 million cycles.

32
00:02:22,834 --> 00:02:28,936
It adds 14 rows of 3819 columns, about 53 thousand cells.

33
00:02:28,986 --> 00:02:31,654
The precompile pays by a wide margin.

34
00:02:32,554 --> 00:02:34,561
Now check it against SHA-3.

35
00:02:34,611 --> 00:02:38,296
The Griffin arm runs 13 percent more cycles.

36
00:02:38,346 --> 00:02:43,296
It also commits 56 million chip cells that the control never pays.

37
00:02:44,196 --> 00:02:49,320
Both terms are larger, so the Griffin arm loses for any cost per cell.

38
00:02:49,370 --> 00:02:52,070
One inequality gives both outcomes.

39
00:02:53,270 --> 00:02:55,995
Could we simply count cycles instead?

40
00:02:56,045 --> 00:02:59,125
A second primitive in PLUM shows why not.

41
00:02:59,175 --> 00:03:01,311
It is the power-residue symbol.

42
00:03:02,211 --> 00:03:06,701
A dedicated chip for it runs in 365 cycles.

43
00:03:06,751 --> 00:03:10,954
The software loop takes 6549.

44
00:03:11,004 --> 00:03:15,297
By cycles, the chip looks 17.9 times cheaper.

45
00:03:16,197 --> 00:03:21,859
By trace area, the chip commits 382 multiplication columns per symbol.

46
00:03:21,909 --> 00:03:24,557
The loop commits 261.

47
00:03:24,607 --> 00:03:27,060
The chip adds area instead of removing it.

48
00:03:27,960 --> 00:03:31,246
The two measures point in opposite directions.

49
00:03:31,296 --> 00:03:35,425
So the rule must be stated in trace area, not in cycles.

50
00:03:36,625 --> 00:03:39,451
Where can removed core area come from?

51
00:03:39,501 --> 00:03:43,696
It comes from emulation, from splitting big numbers into limbs.

52
00:03:44,596 --> 00:03:51,049
With ℓ limbs, one multiplication costs between ℓ and ℓ² native multiplications.

53
00:03:51,099 --> 00:03:53,904
When ℓ is 1, there is nothing to remove.

54
00:03:54,804 --> 00:03:57,925
So the rule predicts something we can test.

55
00:03:57,975 --> 00:04:03,144
A precompile can only pay for a primitive whose field does not match the prover's.

56
00:04:03,194 --> 00:04:06,306
A mismatch is necessary, but not sufficient.

57
00:04:07,506 --> 00:04:11,414
We test the necessary direction first, in execute mode.

58
00:04:11,464 --> 00:04:16,970
The cost per multiplication should be small at 1 limb and grow with the number of limbs.

59
00:04:17,870 --> 00:04:22,144
At 1 limb, a multiplication costs 19 guest cycles.

60
00:04:22,194 --> 00:04:26,318
At 7 limbs, it costs 3121.

61
00:04:26,368 --> 00:04:29,355
That is a factor of 164.

62
00:04:30,255 --> 00:04:37,446
Across 4 widths, 2, 4, 7, and 8 limbs, the cost grows as ℓ to the power 1.30.

63
00:04:37,496 --> 00:04:39,343
That lies inside the band.

64
00:04:40,243 --> 00:04:43,522
Next, three precompiles over the same field.

65
00:04:43,572 --> 00:04:46,979
The Griffin chip is mismatched, and nothing else serves it.

66
00:04:47,029 --> 00:04:52,317
It pays, with 56.4 times fewer cycles in execute mode.

67
00:04:53,217 --> 00:04:56,452
The field-multiplication chip is mismatched too.

68
00:04:56,502 --> 00:05:03,725
But the host already ships a 256-bit multiplication precompile, so it does not pay.

69
00:05:04,625 --> 00:05:07,443
The power-residue chip is the open case.

70
00:05:07,493 --> 00:05:13,480
A fused chip would pay its control overhead once, instead of 261 times.

71
00:05:13,530 --> 00:05:17,916
Whether that wins is an experiment we specify, not one we ran.

72
00:05:18,816 --> 00:05:20,929
One more test is still open.

73
00:05:20,979 --> 00:05:25,998
The rule forecasts that a matched-field precompile will not pay in a full proof.

74
00:05:26,048 --> 00:05:28,760
We state that forecast before the run.

75
00:05:29,960 --> 00:05:35,016
So the precompile makes PLUM verification finite to prove on a laptop.

76
00:05:35,066 --> 00:05:37,702
Is that enough for an anonymous credential?

77
00:05:37,752 --> 00:05:38,631
Not yet.

78
00:05:39,531 --> 00:05:43,556
The proof we can afford is a STARK proof, and it is not zero-knowledge.

79
00:05:43,606 --> 00:05:47,687
The wraps that add zero-knowledge are Groth16 and PLONK.

80
00:05:47,737 --> 00:05:51,430
Both rely on pairings, so neither is post-quantum.

81
00:05:52,330 --> 00:05:55,612
The wrap also fails at recursive compression.

82
00:05:55,662 --> 00:05:59,852
Our Griffin chip overflows the toolchain's fixed recursion shapes.

83
00:05:59,902 --> 00:06:03,584
Regenerating them is a one-time job, not a memory limit.

84
00:06:04,484 --> 00:06:08,781
The zkVM's flexibility does not show up in time either.

85
00:06:08,831 --> 00:06:13,399
Recompiling the static circuit takes about 0.3 seconds.

86
00:06:13,449 --> 00:06:17,578
What differs is how much must be re-audited after a change.

87
00:06:18,778 --> 00:06:23,728
All proving times are at the 80-bit level, which is not a deployment level.

88
00:06:23,778 --> 00:06:28,065
Each is the mean of 3 runs on one 24 gigabyte laptop.

89
00:06:28,965 --> 00:06:33,980
The positive results assume the Griffin circuit's constraints are sound.

90
00:06:34,030 --> 00:06:35,827
That assumption is open.

91
00:06:35,877 --> 00:06:38,680
The negative results do not depend on it.

92
00:06:39,580 --> 00:06:41,472
The practical rule is short.

93
00:06:41,522 --> 00:06:47,130
Before building a precompile, compare the core area it removes with the chip area it adds.

94
00:06:48,030 --> 00:06:50,188
Three questions remain open.

95
00:06:50,238 --> 00:06:56,076
They are the matched-field proof, the fused power-residue chip, and a post-quantum zero-knowledge wrap.

96
00:06:56,126 --> 00:06:59,480
The thesis is called, When does a precompile pay?
